+# 2026-11-11 Does Node 22 global fetch() honour HTTPS_PROXY by default?
+
+## Answer
+
+**No.** In Node 22, global `fetch()` ignores `HTTPS_PROXY`, `HTTP_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` by default.
+
+Since **v22.21.0**, Node can read those variables, but only if you turn it on in one of two ways:
+
+- set `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1`, or
+- start Node with `node --use-env-proxy`.
+
+The feature is marked Stability 1.1 (Active Development). On Node 22 releases before v22.21.0 there is no built-in way to make `fetch()` use these variables. You have to set an undici dispatcher yourself, for example `setGlobalDispatcher(new EnvHttpProxyAgent())` from the `undici` package.
+
+## Evidence
+
+### Node 22 docs: CLI (https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/cli.html)
+
+`--use-env-proxy`, Added in: v22.21.0, Stability: 1.1 – Active Development:
+
+> When enabled, Node.js parses the `HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and `NO_PROXY` environment variables during startup, and tunnels requests over the specified proxy.
+>
+> This is equivalent to setting the `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` environment variable. When both are set, `--use-env-proxy` takes precedence.
+
+The `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` entry has the same wording and the same "Added in: v22.21.0".
+
+The wording "When enabled" means the feature is off unless you turn it on.
+
+### Node 22 docs: HTTP, "Built-in Proxy Support" (https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/http.html#built-in-proxy-support)
+
+Added in: v22.21.0.
+
+> When Node.js creates the global agent, if the `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` environment variable is set to `1` or `--use-env-proxy` is enabled, the global agent will be constructed with `proxyEnv: process.env`, enabling proxy support based on the environment variables.
+
+The `http.Agent` `proxyEnv` option also has **Default: `undefined`**, which means no proxy. Other details from this section:
+
+- Lowercase `http_proxy`, `https_proxy` and `no_proxy` win over the uppercase names.
+- Requests to Unix domain sockets ignore the proxy settings.
+
+### `node --help` (local v22.23.4)
+
+```
+--use-env-proxy   parse proxy settings from HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY/NO_PROXY environment
+                  variables and apply the setting in global HTTP/HTTPS clients
+```
+
+### Local experiment (Node v22.23.4)
+
+`HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY` and their lowercase forms were all set to a local proxy, and `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY` was unset. Then `fetch('http://example.invalid/')` was called.
+
+- **Default run:** the request failed with `ENOTFOUND`. Node tried to resolve DNS directly, so the proxy was not used.
+- **Run with `--use-env-proxy`:** Node printed `[UNDICI-EHPA] Warning: EnvHttpProxyAgent is experimental`. This means the flag makes Node install undici's `EnvHttpProxyAgent`, which is the dispatcher `fetch()` uses. The proxy could not answer in this run, because the test harness blocked the event loop that served it, so the run did not show a full proxied response.
+
+## Caveats / not verified
+
+- I did not read the Node source (`lib/internal/process/pre_execution.js`) because fetching from GitHub was not permitted in this session. The claim that `--use-env-proxy` covers `fetch()` rests on:
+  - the CLI docs, which say "tunnels requests";
+  - `--help`, which says "global HTTP/HTTPS clients";
+  - the `EnvHttpProxyAgent` warning seen in the experiment.
+- The Node 22 docs page for `fetch` was not checked for its own proxy note.
+- Before relying on this, check the exact Node version: `node --version` must be ≥ 22.21.0.
